-- title:
--   Cyclotomic field ℚ(ζ_p^{k+1}) is unramified outside S ni p
-- statement:
--   Let $p$ be a natural number that is prime (as a typeclass fact), let $S$ be a finite set of primes containing the prime $p$ (i.e. the element `pPrime p` of `Nat.Primes`, namely $p$ together with its primality), let $k$ be a natural number, and let $\zeta$ be an element of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}} =$ `AlgebraicClosure ℚ` which is a primitive $p^{k+1}$-th root of unity. The conclusion is that the intermediate field $\mathbb{Q}(\zeta)$ generated by $\zeta$ satisfies `IsUnramifiedOutside S`, which by definition asserts two things: first, $\mathbb{Q}(\zeta)$ is finite-dimensional over $\mathbb{Q}$; second, for every prime $q$ not lying in $S$ and every valuation subring $A$ of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ with $q$ a nonunit of $A$ (i.e. $A$ lies over the rational prime $q$), the inertia subgroup of $A$ over $\mathbb{Q}$, transported into $\mathrm{Gal}(\overline{\mathbb{Q}}/\mathbb{Q}) = \overline{\mathbb{Q}} \simeq_{\mathbb{Q}}^{\mathrm{alg}} \overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ as the image of `A.inertiaSubgroup ℚ` under the inclusion of the decomposition subgroup, is contained in the subgroup of automorphisms fixing $\mathbb{Q}(\zeta)$ pointwise.
--
--   This is the standard fact that a cyclotomic field of $p$-power conductor is ramified only at $p$, recorded in the inertia-theoretic form used by the project's notion of being unramified outside a finite set of primes. It supplies admissible levels $S$ when splitting fields generated by $p$-power roots of unity (for instance $\mathbb{Q}(\zeta_p)$, and $\mathbb{Q}(i)$ when $p = 2$, $k = 1$) are adjoined, and is cited in the construction of unramified-outside-$S$ cohomology and in the finiteness and Herbrand-type arguments built on it.
